<td class=3D"maincell" style=3D"font-family:sans-serif; font-size:14px; ver=
tical-align:top; box-sizing:border-box; padding:20px;">
<p><strong>For regular in-person classes I used to check that every student=
 had all the supplies and one lost 10 points if one did not meet the criter=
ia</strong>. I have found that there is a very high correlation between tho=
se who do not pass Test 0 and lose
 10 points and those who perform very poorly in the course.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>This was Test 0 because the students had to pay very careful att=
ention to the definitions so that the item they procured actually satisfied=
 the defining conditions. This is very good practice for mathematics becaus=
e language is used in a very strict
 and precise manner in mathematics. If we want a computer to do something, =
we must learn to instruct it in a completely unambiguous manner.&nbsp;</str=
ong></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>For online classes, I can neither check anything nor indeed enforce anyt=
hing.&nbsp;&nbsp;<strong>The most important thing is for you to obtain a go=
od ruler for drawing straight lines and also a mechanical pencil with lead =
of appropriate diameter (.5mm, .7mm etc.)
 and softness (2B, 3B, etc.)&nbsp; so that your work is easily visible and =
legible</strong>. This is the case if you print out the exam and write on i=
t. You need to try different things to see if the writing is dark enough an=
d also if you break the lead too frequently.
 Please switch to a thicker lead, if the thinner one breaks too often.<span=
>&nbsp;</span><strong>Usually pencils come with HB lead and, therefore, you=
 will need to replace the lead with 2B, 3b, 4B etc. ALL STRAIGHT LINE SEGME=
NTS OTHER THAN ONES EXPLICITLY EXCLUDED
 MUST BE DRAWN WITH A RULER. ONE WILL LOSE 1 POINT FOR EACH VIOLATION. Plea=
se read the next paragraph if you are using some device to write on a table=
t.</strong></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>I do not have direct knowledge of most of the writing devices that are c=
urrently in use. If one uses a stylus on a tablet of some sort and if one c=
an do everything that one can do with a pencil, a ruler, and a piece of pap=
er, then that should be fine.<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>IT
 IS VERY IMPORTANT THAT ALL<span>&nbsp;</span><span>STRAIGHT LJNES</span><s=
pan>&nbsp;</span>(CONCLUSION BARS, BRACKETS FOR MATRICES AND SO ON) ARE<spa=
n>&nbsp;</span><span>VISIBLY STRAIGHT</span><span>&nbsp;</span>BECAUSE THEY=
 ARE DRAWN WITH A RULER OR SOME SUITABLE SOFTWARE</strong>.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Some pages from student exams with writing done in HB lead are s=
carcely legible</strong>. I instituted the rules precisely because it is ve=
ry difficult to read what students have written if they use HB lead. If you=
 follow the requirements (which,
 I hope you do) then you will become organised before you know. This will s=
ave you a lot of time and many carry these habits into future classes.<span=
>&nbsp;</span><strong>Once you upload your exam please make sure that they =
are legible and that they are not too
 large. You may have to reduce scanning resolution to reduce the size. Let =
me tentatively set the upper limit for the size of an uploaded exam (which =
must be a single document) to 10 MB. If I find a more precise estimate I wi=
ll announce it later</strong>.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>I do not like many of the features on CANVAS; I neither chose the setup =
nor wrote the code. But I can learn the syntax (and the hieroglyphics on MS=
 Outlook) if adequate documentation is provided.<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>=
Test 0 introduces one to the elementary
 mental discipline necessary to make one pay attention to minute details of=
 syntax.&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>In actual fact, I do not have such documentation for CANVAS, but rely on=
 searching on Google to see find an algorithm for doing what I want to do. =
These symbols are conventional and must be defined in order that one know t=
he meaning. I find the claim that
 these are intuitively clear not just preposterous but harmful because it i=
s false and imposes an arbitrary setup (that may be improved upon) under fa=
lse pretenses.&nbsp;<strong>This is precisely why I have, unlike CANVAS, gi=
ven you a list of symbols and their definitions</strong>.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>One can be expected to deduce logical consequences. One cannot be expect=
ed to deduce arbitrary conventions. We shall have occasion to discuss logic=
al implication in Discrete Mathematics.&nbsp;You should consider the follow=
ing question: If you see a picture of
 a human being on a door, does it follow logically that there is a toilet o=
n the other side?</p>
